// Settings shared by every configuration of the app target.
//
// These live here rather than in project.pbxproj so that changing where the
// app points is a one-line diff in a readable file, not a hunt through a
// property list. `Debug.xcconfig` and `Release.xcconfig` include this and then
// differ only in the two things that actually differ: the API host and whether
// cleartext HTTP to a local machine is permitted.

// xcconfig has no string quoting and `//` starts a comment — writing
// `https://host` here silently yields `https:`. Indirecting through a variable
// is the standard way out, and the reason this file looks odd.
SLASH = /

// Set here rather than through Xcode's Signing & Capabilities pane, which would
// write it into project.pbxproj instead. Not a secret — the same ten characters
// are published in the AASA file, which Apple fetches unauthenticated.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = KURWX5TYSZ

// Associated Domains + App Groups. The first is what makes a native passkey
// assertion possible at all; the second is claimed now, unused until C2, so the
// App ID is already provisioned for it when the snapshot cache lands.
CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS = Config/StockHODL.entitlements

// A partial plist: `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE` stays YES and Xcode merges its
// generated keys into this one, so only the keys we genuinely own live here.
INFOPLIST_FILE = Config/Info.plist

// The keychain scope the app and its widget extension SHARE. A widget is a
// separate process: without a common access group it cannot see the session
// token at all, and would render "sign in" beside a signed-in app.
//
// Written with the team ID rather than `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` because this
// value has to reach TWO places — the entitlements file, where the prefix
// variable is available, and Info.plist, whose processing runs before code
// signing has computed it. One literal that both can expand beats two spellings
// that must agree. Not a secret, for the same reason DEVELOPMENT_TEAM above is
// not: the ten characters are published in the AASA file.
KEYCHAIN_ACCESS_GROUP = $(DEVELOPMENT_TEAM).com.robertandrosz.stockhodl.shared

// The keychain item's `kSecAttrService`, shared verbatim by the app and the
// widget extension. Each target's own PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER cannot serve
// this: the extension's is `com.robertandrosz.stockhodl.widgets`, not the
// app's `com.robertandrosz.stockhodl`, and a `KeychainTokenStore` that
// defaulted to `Bundle.main.bundleIdentifier` filed the token under two
// different service strings — reproduced on-device as the app reading the
// token fine while the widget got errSecItemNotFound for it, despite sharing
// the access group. One literal, expanded into both Info.plists below.
KEYCHAIN_SERVICE = com.robertandrosz.stockhodl

// The App Group container, named once. `SnapshotStore.swift` must agree with
// it, and the widget reads the very same file the app writes.
APP_GROUP = group.com.robertandrosz.stockhodl
